{"name":"Sweet Lavender","hex":"#8e8db9","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sweet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Tasting of sugars.","Retaining a portion of sugar."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Used as a positive response to good news or information."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sweet"},{"term":"lavender","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of a group of European plants, genus, Lavandula, of the mint family.","A pale bluish purple or violet colour, like that of the lavender flower."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour.","Pertaining to LGBT people and rights."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lavender"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sweet Lavender","blocks":[{"text":"Sweet Lavender is a play in three acts by Arthur Wing Pinero, first performed in 1888. The sentimental and gently humorous story concerns the romance of Lavender Rolt and Clement Hale, and the complications impeding the course of true love."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/8/86/Sweet-Lavender-1922.tif/lossy-page1-330px-Sweet-Lavender-1922.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweet_Lavender","wikidata":"Q7655388"},{"title":"Sweet Lavender (1920 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Sweet Lavender is a 1920 American silent drama film directed by Paul Powell and starring Mary Miles Minter. The scenario was adapted by Beulah Marie Dix from the 1888 play of the same name by Arthur Wing Pinero. Like many of Minter's features, it is thought to be a lost film."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Sweet_Lavender_1.png/330px-Sweet_Lavender_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweet_Lavender_(1920_film)","wikidata":"Q48672743"},{"title":"Lavandula","blocks":[{"text":"Lavandula is a genus of 47 known species of perennial flowering plants in the sage family, Lamiaceae. It is native to regions of the Iberian Peninsula and Mediterranean coastline, the Middle East, extending to the Indian subcontinent."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Single_lavender_flower02.jpg/330px-Single_lavender_flower02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lavandula","wikidata":"Q171892"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/8/86/Sweet-Lavender-1922.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Sweet-Lavender-1922.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/8/86/Sweet-Lavender-1922.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Sweet-Lavender-1922.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1922 London production of Sweet Lavender. Ann Trevor, far right, played Lavender, and Lilian Braithwaite, next to her, was Mrs Rolt.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASweet-Lavender-1922.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f8/Sweet-Lavender-1888.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Sweet-Lavender-1888.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f8/Sweet-Lavender-1888.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Sweet-Lavender-1888.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Theatre programme from the original run, showing two cast changes since the first night","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASweet-Lavender-1888.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Sweet_Lavender_1.png/330px-Sweet_Lavender_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Sweet_Lavender_1.png/330px-Sweet_Lavender_1.png?utm_source=en